    <!-- CssLayout -->
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.Title1">CSS Layout</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.Sub1">Percentages, aspect-ratio, box-sizing, and position: absolute are handled by the framework's own layout pass — every panel participates, and a custom panel opts in with three lines.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.CC1">Horizontal percentages resolve against the containing block's width, vertical ones against its height — which means the axis has to be determinate.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.CT1">Percentages are resolved, not materialised</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.CC2">All four edges accept percentages. Per CSS, every edge — including the vertical ones — resolves against the containing block's width.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.CT2">Percentage width and height</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.CC3">min-width, max-width, min-height, and max-height take percentages too, and clamp after the preferred size is resolved.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.CT3">Percentage margin and padding</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.CC4">Give one axis and the ratio derives the other. Each tile below sets only a width.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.CT4">Percentage min and max bounds</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.CC5">Both tiles ask for width: 200px with 20px of padding and a 4px border. border-box (the framework default) fits the chrome inside; content-box adds it on top, so the second tile is genuinely wider.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.CT5">aspect-ratio</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.CC6">An absolutely positioned element leaves its panel's flow entirely: it adds nothing to the flow or to the panel's desired size, and the inset properties place it. The host below is an ordinary StackPanel — the flowing items simply ignore the pinned ones.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.CT6">box-sizing</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.CC7">Both bars are inside the same 420-wide block and both ask for width: 50%. The second one also sets Width in markup, which is a local value — and a local value outranks every CSS layer.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.CT7">position: absolute</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.CC8">Every layout feature above, in markup.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.CT8">An explicit size still wins</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.CC9">Opting a custom panel into the absolute-positioning protocol.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.Body1">A percentage cannot become a fixed Width the moment it is parsed — the containing block is only known during measure. So percentage lengths are kept as layout state and resolved against the constraint the parent hands down, every pass. That is what makes them survive a window resize, and it is why a local Width you set in code still outranks a CSS percentage.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.Cap1">A StackPanel hands its children an infinite constraint along the stacking axis, so a vertical percentage there has nothing to resolve against and degrades to auto rather than collapsing to zero. The same applies to a Canvas, and to a horizontal percentage inside a horizontally stacking panel.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.Cap2">StackPanel, WrapPanel, DockPanel, Grid, and Canvas all participate. A custom panel joins in by skipping absolute children in its loops (if (IsCssAbsolute(child)) continue;) and calling the two MeasureCssAbsoluteChildren / ArrangeCssAbsoluteChildren helpers. Without position: absolute, pixel left/top keep their historical Canvas attached-property mapping.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.Note1">Widths inside a fixed 420-wide block</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.Note2">Heights need a determinate constraint</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.Tile1">height: 60%&#x0a;of a Grid cell</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.Tile2">the same 60% in a&#x0a;StackPanel — degrades&#x0a;to auto</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.Tile3">margin: 10%&#x0a;→ 20 DIP on every edge</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.Tile4">width: 90% clamped by max-width: 50%</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.Tile5">width: 10% lifted by min-width: 40%</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.Tile6">2 / 3&#x0a;(height given)</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.Tile7">border-box → 200 total</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.Tile8">Normal flow item</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.Tile9">Another flow item — unaffected by the pinned boxes</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.Tile10">top + right</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.Tile11">left + right + bottom  →  stretches between both edges</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.Tile12">left/top&#x0a;in %</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssLayout.Tile13">Width=&quot;140&quot; beats it</x:String>

    <!-- CssMappings -->
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.Title1">Custom Properties</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.Sub1">Both halves of the mapping table are open: register your own property names, your own value converters, or redirect a name to something that already exists. Every declaration on this page uses a custom property that does not exist in CSS.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.CC1">brand-corner is registered as an alias of border-radius. It behaves as that longhand in every respect, including how it competes in the cascade.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.CT1">Four ways to extend the table</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.CC2">gallery-elevation takes a level from 0 to 5 and builds a matching drop shadow. Nothing about it exists in CSS or in the framework — it is thirty lines of registration.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.CT2">An alias for a design token</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.CC3">gallery-tint takes any CSS color and paints it back at 35% alpha — the alpha comes from the registration's converter parameter — resolving Background against whichever type the element actually is.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.CT3">A custom property with a custom converter</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.CC4">The parameter belongs to the registration, not to the declaration. gallery-tint and gallery-tint-strong are the same converter instance registered twice — only the alpha passed at registration differs.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.CT4">Resolving the target by name</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.CC5">A multi-property converter sees the element and returns a list of assignments. gallery-square writes both Width and Height from a single length.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.CT5">One converter, two registrations</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.CC6">A name CSS has no keyword for falls back to the element's own properties, converted from kebab-case. No registration needed: horizontal-content-alignment finds HorizontalContentAlignment, is-tab-stop finds IsTabStop.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.CT6">One declaration, several properties</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.CC7">A panel overrides TryApplyCssPropertyCore to claim a CSS concept and map it onto its own properties. StackPanel does exactly that for flex-direction, so a stack responds to a flexbox keyword it has no other notion of.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.CT7">The kebab-case channel — every dependency property, for free</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.CC8">Custom properties read exactly like built-in ones.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.CT8">Panels can intercept a property themselves</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.CC9">The registrations behind this page, verbatim.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.Body1">CssMappings.RegisterAlias points one name at another. RegisterProperty binds a name to a fixed dependency property, or resolves the target by name against each element's type, or hands the whole job to a multi-property converter that can assign several properties at once. Value conversion is interface-shaped — ICssValueConverter is modelled on IValueConverter but narrowed for CSS: the input is always the declaration text, conversion is one-way, and the culture is always invariant. Later registrations win — including over built-in entries and over the unsupported placeholders — and style sheets that were already parsed pick up new registrations automatically.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.Cap1">A converter returning null drops that declaration and lets the rest of the block apply — the CSS error model, not an exception. By default the conversion runs once per distinct declaration text and the result is shared across every matched element, so return frozen objects; pass cacheByValue: false to convert on every application instead.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.Cap2">CssValueParsing exposes the engine's own color and length parsing to custom converters, so a hand-written property understands exactly the same syntax as a built-in one — hex with trailing alpha, named colors, and rgb()/hsl() in both syntaxes.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.Cap3">A converter also receives the target property&apos;s type, and a culture that is always invariant — style-sheet text is culture-invariant source code, and a cached conversion is shared process-wide, so it must not depend on the thread that happened to compile it.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.Cap4">Values go through the same converter stack XAML uses, so enums, booleans, and anything with a type converter all work. Real CSS properties the framework cannot honour are registered as explicit unsupported entries instead, so they never reach this channel by accident and mis-target a same-named property.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.Cap5">The override writes through the setter it is handed, so the value lands in the CSS layers and is cleared automatically when the rule stops matching. The same hook is the last resort for any unregistered property name, which lets a custom panel consume arbitrary keys of its own.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.Note1">StackPanel with flex-direction: row</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.Note2">The same markup with flex-direction: column</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.Tile1">9 — out of&#x0a;range, dropped</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.Tile2">horizontal-alignment: Right</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssMappings.Tile3">text-wrapping: Wrap  ·  font-weight comes from real CSS</x:String>

    <!-- CssOutline -->
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.Title1">Outline</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.Sub1">A ring drawn outside an element's bounds that takes no layout space. It is a first-class part of the framework — four dependency properties usable from XAML or code — and the CSS outline family maps straight onto it.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.CC1">outline-style takes none, solid, dashed, or dotted. The shorthand accepts the three parts in any order.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.CT1">Why not just a border?</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.CC2">Positive values push the ring away from the element; negative values pull it inside. None of it changes the element's size.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.CT2">Width, color, and style</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.CC3">A rounded element gets a rounded outline, expanded by the offset so the curve stays concentric.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.CT3">outline-offset</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.CC4">Both rows hold three identical tiles. The top row adds an outline to the middle tile, the bottom row adds a border of the same width — watch the row shift.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.CT4">The ring follows the corner radius</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.CC5">Click a button, then press Tab. :focus-visible only matches keyboard focus, so the ring appears for the keyboard and stays out of the way for the mouse — the whole pattern is two lines of CSS.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.CT5">Outline costs no layout, a border does</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.CC6">The four properties live on FrameworkElement, so they bind, animate, and take part in styles and triggers like anything else.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.CT6">The focus ring it was made for</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.CC7">Both channels: the CSS shorthand and the properties directly.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.CT7">Without any CSS at all</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.CC8">A validation halo driven from code.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.Body1">A border is part of the box: growing one reflows everything around it. An outline is painted after the element renders, outside its bounds, and never participates in measure or arrange. That is what makes it the right tool for a focus ring, a validation halo, or a selection marker — the layout does not shift the moment the state appears.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.Cap1">Because the ring is drawn after the element's own effects and outside its bounds, it survives clipping by a parent's rounded corners and is not blurred by an effect on the element itself.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.Cap2">OutlineStyle defaults to Solid, so setting a brush and a thickness is enough to see a ring. In CSS the shorthand always resets all three parts, which means outline: 2px red is invisible until a style is named — the same rule browsers apply — and outline: none clears everything.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.Note1">With an outline — its neighbours do not move</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssOutline.Note2">With a border — everything after it is pushed along</x:String>

    <!-- CssSelectors -->
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Title1">Selectors &amp; Cascade</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.ScopeTitle">Nesting and scope boundaries</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.NamespaceTitle">XAML namespaces in CSS</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.NamespaceCaption">The same Border control uses two XAML namespace names. CSS selects each expanded name separately. Change the text to remove the qualified Tag highlight while its native binding stays active.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.ScopeCaption">Nested rules color the cards inside @scope. Disabling the lower group makes it a scope limit, so its card returns to gray. IsEnabled remains connected through a native XAML binding.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.ScopeToggle">Enable the lower group</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.ScopeInside">Always inside the scope</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.ScopeConditional">Scope ends at a disabled group</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Sub1">Type, class, id, and universal selectors with descendant and child combinators, the dynamic pseudo-classes, and the cascade rules that decide which declaration wins.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.CC1">A type selector matches derived types too, so Control catches every Button. An id selector matches x:Name.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.CT1">Selector kinds</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.CC2">A B matches any B under an A at any depth; A > B matches only a direct child. Both walk the logical tree.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.CT2">Descendant and child combinators</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.CC3">Hover, press, and tab through the controls below — every visual change here comes from a pseudo-class rule, not from a trigger.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.CT3">Dynamic pseudo-classes</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.CC4">Every Border below is matched by several rules at once. The winner follows the standard CSS cascade: scope, then specificity, then document order.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.CT4">Specificity and document order</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.CC5">An important declaration is protected from a more specific normal one, exactly as on the web.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.CT5">!important</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.CC6">CSS values live in two dedicated layers. They override style setters — theme, implicit, and explicit — but never a local value you set yourself.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.CT6">Where CSS sits in the property system</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.CC7">Declaring a transition makes every state change animate. Hover the two panes to compare.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.CT7">Transitions on pseudo-class flips</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.CC8">Elements generated by a control template never match page-level selectors, and the ancestor axis skips them — so Button > TextBlock reaches the button's content even though template elements sit between them visually.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.CT8">Template parts are invisible to page CSS</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.CC9">The selector demos above, declared in markup.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.CC10">Sheets, classes, and reading back what the cascade decided.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Cap1">Sibling combinators (~ and +), attribute selectors, pseudo-elements, and functional pseudo-classes such as :not() are not supported; rules using them are dropped with a diagnostic rather than silently mismatching.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Cap2">:hover  :active  :focus  :focus-visible  :focus-within  :enabled  :disabled  :checked  :indeterminate</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Cap3">local value  &gt;  template triggers  &gt;  CSS :state winners  &gt;  style triggers  &gt;  template triggers  &gt;  CSS normal winners  &gt;  style setters  &gt;  default</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Cap4">One shared duration and curve per element; per-property delays are ignored with a diagnostic.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Cap5">Without that rule the button's own template chrome would be a styling target, and every theme would break the moment a page wrote a type selector. Content you author, including DataTemplate output, stays fully matchable.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Note1">* { margin: 0 0 8px 0 }  — the universal selector reaches this bare TextBlock too</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Tile1">Border { … }  — type selector</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Tile2">.tinted { … }  — class selector</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Tile3">#named { … }  — id selector, matches x:Name</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Tile4">Direct child — .outer &gt; TextBlock wins (green, bold)</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Tile5">Nested deeper — only .outer TextBlock matches (amber)</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Tile6">Hover / press me</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Tile7">Tab here for :focus-visible</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Tile8">Toggle me — :checked flips the label</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Tile9">Border only  →  specificity 0,0,1  →  slate</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Tile10">#winner  →  1,0,0 beats every class  →  violet</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Tile11">#louder has higher specificity, but the type rule is !important — rose wins.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Tile12">No local value  →  the CSS background applies (blue)</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Tile13">Background set locally in markup  →  the local value keeps winning (brown)</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Tile14">No transition&#x0a;— snaps instantly</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Tile15">transition: 0.35s ease-in-out&#x0a;— eases both ways</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssSelectors.Tile16">Content TextBlock — matched (amber)</x:String>

    <!-- CssStyling -->
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.Title1">CSS Styling</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.PropertyTitle">Typed custom properties</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.MathTitle">Math functions with native bindings</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.FontFaceCaption">Ahem loads from an embedded font resource and renders predictable square glyphs. Edit the text; the original Text binding and native 20px FontSize remain in control.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.RelativeUnitTitle">Font and viewport rulers</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.RelativeUnitCaption">The font-size slider above also resizes this 12ch × 2lh card. The slider below changes an embedded host's dynamic viewport while its small and large sizes stay fixed.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.MathCaption">The slider controls the native FontSize binding. CSS rounds the width to 40px steps and computes opacity from the current font size. Changing the font recomputes both values.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.ImportTitle">Conditional stylesheet imports</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.ImportCaption">Embedded CSS files load into base and responsive layers through @import. The responsive import requires Grid support and a 600px viewport; its container query switches columns as you resize the card.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.PropertyCaption">Edit the width and color declarations. Invalid values return to the registered 160px width and blue color. The declaration text stays connected through a native XAML binding.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.Sub1">Style any element with real CSS. Property keywords are 100% native CSS; anything CSS has no keyword for reaches the control's dependency properties through an automatic kebab-case name.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.CC1">Css.Style takes a declaration block, exactly as it would appear in a style attribute on the web.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.CT1">Two channels, one engine</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.CC2">Css.StyleSheet holds rules scoped to this element's subtree — the markup equivalent of a &lt;style&gt; block. Every Border below is unstyled in markup; the sheet does all the work.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.CT2">Inline declarations</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.CC3">px (the default when a number carries no unit), pt, in, cm, mm, q, pc, plus the font-relative em and rem. Every bar below asks for the same visual width written a different way.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.CT3">A style sheet in markup</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.CC4">Hex with CSS trailing-alpha semantics, every CSS named color, rgb()/rgba()/hsl()/hsla() in both legacy and modern syntax, and transparent.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.CT4">Length units</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.CC5">Where CSS and the framework disagree, the engine converts rather than inventing a keyword.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.CT5">Color syntax</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.CC6">CSS never throws. A malformed declaration is dropped and the rest of the block still applies — the same error model browsers use.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.CT6">How CSS names reach the property system</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.CC7">Both channels declared in markup.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.CT7">When a property cannot apply</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.CC8">The same engine from code, including application-wide sheets.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.Body1">Css.Style carries a declaration block on a single element. Css.StyleSheet (or Css.StyleSheets in code) carries selector rules scoped to a subtree, and Application.StyleSheets carries them app-wide. Both channels feed the same cascade and land in dedicated value layers that sit above style setters but below a local value you set yourself.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.Cap1">em resolves against the element's own font-size, except on font-size itself, where CSS says it resolves against the inherited size.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.Cap2">#RRGGBBAA puts alpha last, per CSS — the opposite of XAML's #AARRGGBB, so the CSS channel parses colors on its own instead of borrowing the XAML converter.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.Cap3">Real CSS properties the framework has no capability for — letter-spacing, text-shadow, animation, backdrop-filter and friends — are registered as explicit unsupported entries. They never silently fall through to the kebab-case channel, and each reports a one-shot diagnostic naming the nearest alternative. Set CssDiagnostics.LogUnresolvedProperties = false to silence them.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.Chip1">Css.Style</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.Chip2">Css.StyleSheet</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.Chip3">Css.Class</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.Chip4">Application.StyleSheets</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.Tile1">rgb() modern syntax&#x0a;pill radius</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.Tile2">hsl()&#x0a;border shorthand</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.Tile3">linear-gradient()&#x0a;CSS angle semantics</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.Tile4">box-shadow&#x0a;→ DropShadowEffect</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.Tile5">transform&#x0a;no layout effect</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.Tile6">#hero — id selector matches x:Name</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.Tile7">Border — type selector, matches derived types too</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.Tile8">.accent — class selector via Css.Class</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.Tile9">.warn — a second class in the same sheet</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.CssStyling.Tile10">The first background-color is invalid and dropped; the later valid one wins.</x:String>

    <!-- DataGrid -->
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.DataGrid.Title1">DataGrid</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.DataGrid.Sub1">A control that presents a collection of data in a customizable grid of rows and columns, with sorting, resizing, selection and auto-generated columns.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.DataGrid.CC1">Click a row to select it, and click a column header to sort.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.DataGrid.CC2">A quick tour of what the grid supports out of the box.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.DataGrid.CC3">Declarative markup for the grid above.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.DataGrid.CC4">Populating the grid and handling selection in code-behind.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.DataGrid.Body1">•  Auto-generate columns from the data source</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.DataGrid.Body2">•  Column sorting (click headers)</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.DataGrid.Body3">•  Column resizing (drag column edges)</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.DataGrid.Body4">•  Single and Extended selection modes</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.DataGrid.Body5">•  Row and column header visibility</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.DataGrid.Body6">•  Alternating row backgrounds</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.DataGrid.Body7">•  Grid lines customization</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.DataGrid.Body8">•  Keyboard navigation (Up / Down / Home / End)</x:String>

    <!-- DatePicker -->
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.DatePicker.Title1">DatePicker</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.DatePicker.Sub1">A control that lets users pick a date from a drop-down calendar, with configurable display formats and optional range constraints.</x:String>
