    <!-- Slider -->
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Slider.Title1">Slider</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Slider.Sub1">A control that lets people pick a value from a range by dragging a thumb along a track, with optional tick marks and snapping.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Slider.CC1">Drag the thumb to change the value and watch the readout update live.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Slider.CT1">Slider with ticks</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Slider.CC2">Tick marks placed at a regular TickFrequency hint at the available stops along the track.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Slider.CT2">Custom range</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Slider.CC3">With IsSnapToTickEnabled the thumb locks onto the nearest tick position as you drag.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Slider.CC4">TrackMode=&quot;Segmented&quot; splits the track into one pill per tick interval. The stops come from Ticks when it is set, otherwise from TickFrequency, and SegmentGap controls the spacing carved out between them.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Slider.CC5">Set any Minimum and Maximum — including negative ranges — to match the data you are bound to.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Slider.CC6">Declarative markup for the sliders above.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Slider.CC7">Handling ValueChanged and building sliders in code-behind.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Slider.Cap1">TickFrequency = 20, snapped — the value always lands on a segment boundary</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Slider.Cap2">Without snapping the segment under the thumb fills proportionally</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Slider.Cap3">Uneven stops via Ticks = 10, 30, 60 with a wider SegmentGap</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Slider.Val1">Volume (0-100)</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Slider.Val2">Temperature (-10 to 40)</x:String>

    <!-- Sparkline -->
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Sparkline.Title1">Sparkline</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Sparkline.Sub1">A lightweight inline chart that condenses a data series into a compact, word-sized graphic — rendered without axes, gridlines, or legends.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Sparkline.CC1">Multiple sparklines using the Line type with high/low point markers.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Sparkline.CT1">Line Sparklines</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Sparkline.CC2">Sparklines rendered as bar charts and area fills.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Sparkline.CT2">Bar &amp; Area</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Sparkline.CC3">Win/Loss sparklines showing positive and negative outcomes.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Sparkline.CT3">Win/Loss</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Sparkline.CC4">Declarative markup for the sparkline variants shown above.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.Sparkline.CC5">The ViewModel supplies deterministic series; all Sparkline variants are declared in XAML.</x:String>

    <!-- SplitButton -->
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.SplitButton.Title1">SplitButton</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.SplitButton.Sub1">A control with a primary action and a drop-down button that opens a Flyout.</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="Loc.P.SplitButton.CC1">Primary click runs the action. Drop-down selects the target.</x:String>
